    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d258">

                <pb facs="0569" n="541" xml:id="pg541"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.90/8–1654</note>
                <head>No. 258<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Assistant Secretary of
                        State for Far Eastern Affairs (Robertson) to the Secretary of
                        State</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d258-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1954-08-19">August 19, 1954</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>Subject:</label>
                    <item>Ambassador Allison’s Telegram No. 374 (Tab A)<anchor n="2"
                            xml:id="d258-f2"/></item>
                </list>
                <p>The developments discussed in Ambassador Allison’s telegram No. 374 on which
                    additional information appears necessary are discussed separately in the four
                        attachments<anchor n="3" xml:id="d258-f3"/> listed below. You will note that
                    in each case our information indicates that Ambassador Allison’s conclusions may
                    be somewhat extreme.</p>




                <xi:include href="d258-a1.xml" xpointer="d258-a1"/>
                <xi:include href="d258-a2.xml" xpointer="d258-a2"/>
                <xi:include href="d258-a3.xml" xpointer="d258-a3"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d258-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d258-f1-content">
                <p>A handwritten notation on the source text by Roderic O’Connor indicates that the
                    memorandum was seen by Secretary Dulles.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d258-f2" n="2" xml:id="d258-f2-content">
                <p><ref target="#d253">Document 253</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d258-f3" n="3" xml:id="d258-f3-content">
                <p>The four attachments, all unsigned and undated, were in effect component parts of
                    Robertson’s memorandum, and the three here printed are so treated. The third,
                    headed “Rastvorov Case,” is not printed.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
